Feature: Battery and power
  The T1-S VTOL flies on a 4S pack. A hover uses much more power than forward flight,
  because the three rotors carry the whole weight. In forward flight the wing carries the
  weight, and only the two wing motors turn at a low speed. The BATTERY line on the SYSTEMS
  screen shows the voltage and the current of the pack.

  Expectation: A hover draws 15 to 25 A
    Given the vehicle is armed
    And the plane is on the ground
    When the operator takes off vertically
    Then the flight is hovering
    And the battery current is at least 15 A
    And the battery current is at most 25 A

  Expectation: The load pulls the voltage down
    A full 4S high-voltage pack reads 17.4 V with no load. A hover draws a large current,
    so the voltage drops below that. It stays above 14 V, which is 3.5 V for each cell.
    Given the vehicle is armed
    And the plane is on the ground
    When the operator takes off vertically
    Then the battery voltage is at most 17 V
    And the battery voltage is at least 14 V

  Expectation: Cruise draws 3 to 7 A
    Given the flight is hovering
    When the operator switches to forward flight
    Then the flight is cruising
    And the battery current is at least 3 A
    And the battery current is at most 7 A
